  "title": "Human Responses to Visually Evoked Threat: intracranial EEG during virtual-reality heights, no-heights and shark stimuli (3 patients)",
  "description": "Intracranial EEG recordings from three patients with treatment-resistant epilepsy who viewed immersive virtual-reality stimuli: a modified virtual-heights scene, a no-heights baseline in a virtual hospital room, and a 360-degree great white shark movie. Skin conductance and visual-scanning measures were recorded alongside the iEEG. Electrodes covered regions of interest including the insula and orbitofrontal cortex. The data support study of the neural basis of visually evoked threat and anxiety responses.",
  "methods_description": "iEEG was recorded at UCSF with Tucker-Davis Technologies PZ2/PZ5 amplifiers and an RZ2 acquisition system at 3051.76 Hz, using depth electrodes, a grid and strips. Stimuli were Unity-programmed VR scenes shown on an HTC Vive headset. Raw TDT MATLAB exports were converted to BrainVision float32 with no filtering, resampling or re-referencing. Electrodes were localized by co-registering pre-operative MRI with post-operative CT.",
